The Perils of Playing the “How Far Can I Go?” Game

 1. **The Fuel Pump’s Cry for Help** Your fuel pump is like the unsung hero of your car, quietly working away to deliver fuel from the tank to the engine. When you drive on “E,” you’re essentially making this hero work overtime with minimal resources. Imagine trying to suck the last drops of a milkshake through a straw – that’s what your fuel pump is dealing with. Over time, this can lead to overheating and early retirement for your trusty pump, costing you a pretty penny in repairs.

2. **Sediment Shenanigans** Sediment at the bottom of your fuel tank is like the unwelcome party guest who refuses to leave. Normally, it just sits there, minding its own business. But when you’re running on fumes, your car starts gulping down the dregs of the tank, pulling that sediment along for the ride. This can clog up your fuel filter and injectors, leading to poor performance and a grumpy engine. And trust us, you don’t want to mess with a grumpy engine.

3. **The Engine’s Empty Stomach** Running out of gas isn’t just a minor inconvenience; it can lead to engine damage. When your car’s fuel system is starved, the engine can misfire or stall, causing potential damage to internal components. It’s like trying to run a marathon on an empty stomach – it’s not going to end well. Keep your engine happy and avoid these hangry moments by ensuring it never goes hungry.

4. **Strain on the Battery** When your car stalls due to lack of fuel, it puts extra strain on the battery to restart the engine. If this happens frequently, your battery might decide it’s had enough and give up entirely. Replacing a battery is no joke, especially when it could have been avoided with a timely refuel.

