--- More Than a Franchise: Why We Created the Red, White & Fuel Veteran Franchise Award ---
URL: https://v1.stageofproject.com/juicedfuel/why-we-created-the-red-white-fuel-veteran-franchise-award/
When people hear “franchise giveaway,” they usually picture a sweepstakes or random drawing.

That’s not what this is.

The Red, White & Fuel Veteran Franchise Award was created because we believe military service develops some of the strongest leaders in the world. Leadership, discipline, resilience, accountability, and the ability to adapt under pressure aren’t just qualities that help someone serve our country. They’re the same qualities that help someone build a successful business.

As a veteran-owned company, this mission is personal.

Before Juiced Fuel ever existed, my husband and business partner, Korey, served as an Apache helicopter pilot in the U.S. Army, deploying in support of Operation Iraqi Freedom before continuing his service with the South Carolina Army National Guard. The leadership skills he developed in the military became the foundation for how we built Juiced Fuel from the ground up.

Over the years, we’ve met hundreds of veterans who possess incredible leadership ability but simply need the right opportunity to take the next step. Some are transitioning out of the military. Others have spent years building careers in corporate America. Some have dreamed of owning a business but weren’t sure where to begin.

That’s why the Fuel Forward Foundation partnered with Juiced Fuel Franchising LLC to create the Red, White & Fuel Veteran Franchise Award.

This program isn’t about giving someone a business.

It’s about investing in someone who has already demonstrated the character, commitment, and work ethic to build one.

The selected recipient will receive the initial Juiced Fuel franchise fee, but every candidate will earn something even more valuable: the opportunity to explore franchise ownership through a structured selection process designed to prepare them for long-term success.

Every application is carefully reviewed. Qualified candidates will progress through multiple stages, including a franchise readiness questionnaire, introductory franchise discussions, financial and operational education, Discovery Day, and an independent judging process. We intentionally designed this program to identify leaders who are ready for the responsibility of business ownership, not simply those with the best application.

One of the things we’re most excited about is that veterans don’t even have to nominate themselves.

We know there are incredible leaders who would never raise their own hand. That’s why we’ve opened the door for spouses, family members, friends, employers, battle buddies, and community members to nominate someone they believe deserves this opportunity.

Sometimes the people around us see our potential before we do.

Whether you’re a veteran searching for your next mission or someone who knows a veteran ready to lead a business of their own, we hope you’ll take a closer look at the Red, White & Fuel Veteran Franchise Award.

Because this isn’t just about building another franchise.

It’s about investing in the people who have already invested so much in our country.

If you know someone who has served with honor, leads with integrity, and is ready to build something lasting, we invite you to nominate them or encourage them to apply.

Their next mission may be closer than they think.

--- Why Military Service Builds Exceptional Business Owners ---
URL: https://v1.stageofproject.com/juicedfuel/why-military-service-builds-exceptional-business-owners/
Ask ten veterans what they learned during their time in the military, and you’ll probably hear ten different answers. build with jploft 

Leadership.

Discipline.

Teamwork.

Problem solving.

Resilience.

Accountability.

What you probably won’t hear is, “The military prepared me to own a business.”

But maybe it did.

When we started building Juiced Fuel, we quickly realized that many of the same principles that make someone successful in military service also make them successful in entrepreneurship.

In the military, you’re taught to make decisions under pressure. You learn how to adapt when plans change. You understand that every mission depends on preparation, communication, and execution. You become comfortable taking ownership, solving problems, and putting the team before yourself.

Sound familiar?

That’s exactly what running a business looks like.

Every day as a business owner brings a new challenge. Equipment breaks. Customers have questions. Markets shift. Plans change. Success isn’t about avoiding problems. It’s about learning how to respond when they happen.

Military service teaches that mindset better than almost anything else.

One of the qualities we admire most about veterans is their ability to work within a proven system while still thinking independently. Franchising works the same way. A franchise provides the playbook, but it’s the owner who brings the leadership, work ethic, and commitment to execute it every day.

At Juiced Fuel, we’ve also learned that leadership isn’t about rank.

Some of the best leaders we’ve met aren’t the loudest person in the room. They’re the ones who show up consistently, take responsibility, support their team, and stay focused on the mission when things get difficult.

That’s exactly the kind of person we’re hoping to find through the Red, White & Fuel Veteran Franchise Award.

This program isn’t looking for the most decorated service record or the longest résumé. It’s looking for people who have demonstrated integrity, resilience, accountability, and the desire to continue serving their communities in a new way.

For some, that next mission may be building a business that creates jobs.

For others, it may be serving customers, mentoring employees, or providing opportunities for future generations.

Entrepreneurship isn’t a replacement for military service.

It’s simply another opportunity to lead.

If you’ve spent your career serving something bigger than yourself, maybe your next mission isn’t behind you.

Maybe it’s just getting started.

If you’re a veteran who has ever wondered whether business ownership is possible, or you know someone who embodies these qualities, we encourage you to learn more about the Red, White & Fuel Veteran Franchise Award.

Leadership doesn’t end when military service does.

Sometimes it just takes on a new uniform.

--- The Mission Doesn’t End When the Uniform Comes Off ---
URL: https://v1.stageofproject.com/juicedfuel/the-mission-doesnt-end-when-the-uniform-comes-off/
For many service members, military life provides something that can’t easily be replaced.

A mission.

A team.

A purpose bigger than yourself.

Then one day, the uniform comes off.

For some, the transition into civilian life is exciting. For others, it’s challenging. Not because they aren’t capable, but because they’re searching for that same sense of purpose they once had.

Many veterans continue serving in different ways. They become parents, coaches, volunteers, first responders, community leaders, and business owners. The mission changes, but the desire to lead and make a difference doesn’t.

We’ve seen that firsthand.

At Juiced Fuel, we’ve met veterans from every branch of the military and every stage of life. Some were just beginning their transition. Others had spent years in corporate America. Some had already built successful careers but still felt called to create something of their own.

One thing they all had in common was a willingness to serve.

That’s one of the reasons the Red, White & Fuel Veteran Franchise Award means so much to us.

This program isn’t about replacing military service.

Nothing ever could.

It’s about creating another opportunity to lead.

Owning a business means serving customers with integrity. It means creating jobs, mentoring employees, supporting your local community, and building something your family can be proud of. Leadership simply takes on a different form.

For Korey, our Co-Founder and President, that transition was personal. The values he learned as an Army Apache helicopter pilot—discipline, accountability, preparation, and putting the mission first—didn’t stay in the military. They became the foundation for how we built Juiced Fuel.

Those same values continue to shape our company every day.

That’s why this award isn’t based on luck.

We’re looking for individuals who have demonstrated character, resilience, leadership, and a genuine desire to continue making an impact. People who understand that success isn’t measured only by revenue, but by the lives they influence along the way.

The next mission doesn’t have to look like the last one.

Sometimes it looks like opening the doors to your own business.

Sometimes it looks like creating opportunities for others.

Sometimes it looks like building something that outlives you.

If you’re a veteran wondering what’s next, know this:

The leadership that carried you through military service hasn’t disappeared.

Your experience still matters.

Your skills still matter.

Your ability to lead still matters.

And while one chapter may have ended, another may just be beginning.

The mission doesn’t end when the uniform comes off.

Sometimes it simply changes its address.

