There are currently no marketing cooperatives, purchasing cooperatives, or other
cooperatives that impose fees on you.

Notes

1.     “Adjusted Gross Sales” is defined in our franchise agreement as the gross total
dollar amount of all sales generated through your business for a given period, including, but not
limited to, payment for any service or products sold by you, whether for cash or credit, and also
including any proceeds you receive from business interruption insurance. The gross total dollar
amount of all sales generated does not include (i) bona fide refunds to customers, (ii) sales
allowances and discounts, (iii) sales taxes collected, or (iv) sales of prepaid cards or similar
products (but the redemption of any such card or product will be included in the gross total dollar
amount of all sales).

2.      Beginning with the first full calendar month after (i) the date you open for business
or (ii) 180 days after your franchise agreement is fully signed, you must pay us a minimum monthly
for your first territory equal to (A) $1,000 per month for the first six months, (B) $1,500 per month
for months 7 to 12, (C) $2,000 per month for months 13 to 18, and (D) $2,500 per month for the
19th month and each month thereafter through the end of your term.

If you have multiple territories, then every twelve months, each additional territory will be
subject to the same minimum, with the same ramp-up period. We calculate the minimum monthly
as a total for all your territories, rather than being a minimum you must pay separately for each
territory. In other words, if your royalties based on adjusted gross sales exceed the minimum in
one territory but fall short in a second territory, the excess in the first territory is credited toward
the second territory.

The following chart shows the minimum monthly royalty for franchisees with one to five
territories:
pay separately for each
territory. In other words, if your royalties based on adjusted gross sales exceed the minimum in
one territory but fall short in a second territory, the excess in the first territory is credited toward
the second territory.

